// The Test for the `Event` Process. Its expectations are stated in `modules/process/event/event.md`
// under What an Event Is and What an Event Is Not, and are cited with each one below; nothing here
// is stated from the sources, and where a source and this document disagree the disagreement is
// this Test's finding rather than a change to either.
//
// The language this module is written in has no runner and no assertion form, so an expectation
// here is written as what must hold together with the observation that would decide it, which is
// the form the Test protocol gives an expectation the domain cannot execute. Nothing in this file
// has been observed; each expectation is open until an observer reports what the observation found.

// E1 — An Event composes `Observer_DataType`, `Observer_Time`, and `Logic_Equivalence`, and nothing
// else the protocol requires.
// Stated at: `modules/process/event/event.md` -> What an Event Is, which shows the composition as
// `Observer_DataType`, `Observer_Time`, `Logic_Equivalence`.
// Decided by: open `modules/process/event/protocol/event.kh` and read the block of `tp Event ab`.
// Three entries decide for E1. A fourth entry decides against it, whatever the fourth names.
// A block that lists fewer than three decides against it.

// E2 — Every member of that composition is an abstraction, so the base contract names behavior and
// no type identity.
// Stated at: `modules/process/event/event.md` -> What an Event Is, which grounds the composition in
// `docs/khayyam/abstraction.md` -> Behavior Over Type Identity; and What an Event Is Not, whose third
// separation refuses the composition any representation.
// Decided by: for each entry of the block E1 read, follow its `in` to the file it names and read the
// kind of the declaration behind that name. Three `ab` declarations decide for E2. Any entry whose
// target declares a `cp` decides against it.

// E3 — No capsule composes `Event`; a Type dispatches an Event rather than holding one.
// Stated at: `modules/process/event/event.md` -> What an Event Is, which says composing the
// abstraction is what makes the fact dispatchable and is not a claim that the fact is held anywhere.
// Decided by: read every `tp {name} cp { … }` block under `modules/` and look for the name `Event`
// among the entries. No such block decides for E3. One such block decides against it, whether or not
// the capsule behind it dispatches anything.

// E4 — The `Event` capsule satisfies the `Event` abstraction.
// Stated at: `modules/process/event/event.md` -> What an Event Is, for the identity and time a
// receiver is promised; the satisfaction itself is `docs/khayyam/abstraction.md` -> Abstraction
// Realization, which judges it by method name and receiver and not by where either was written.
// Decided by: read `modules/process/event/event.kh` for the methods whose owner group names `Event`,
// then read `modules/computer/type/protocol/type.observers.kh` and
// `modules/time/protocol/time.observers.kh` for the two each names. A method on `self Event` named
// as those two name them, with the same number of influencing variables and the same influenced
// variables and their types in order, decides for that member. The receiver differing from the
// abstraction's own receiver is what that document calls "by design" and is not a failure.

// E5 — Two occurrences of one kind of event are comparable by the method `Logic_Equivalence` names,
// and this expectation cannot be stated as a checkable claim until one is named.
// Stated at: `modules/process/event/event.md` -> What an Event Is, which composes `Logic_Equivalence`
// "to let a receiver compare two occurrences", and What this protocol does not claim, which declines
// to say what that comparison answers.
// Decided by: none available. `modules/math/logic/protocol/equivalence.kh` declares `Logic_Equivalence`
// and no method, so no realization has anything to implement and no observation can decide whether a
// receiver can compare two occurrences. The spelling the method takes and what it compares are open
// at `modules/process/event/event.handoff.md` -> What does `Logic_Equivalence` over two Event
// occurrences answer? An observer who has read that answer states this expectation in the form it
// then takes, and E4 decides it with the two accessors above.
